WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.900
One lawsuit seeks to block Alaska voters' Senate result if Dan Sullivan loses. This is Omni View North America, episode 26. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. After that, an Ohio ad fight, letters to California election officials, and quiet firings inside an Alberta group, then staffing rules in Canadian nursing homes, a short run of other news, and Canada at a nature summit in Armenia. The Justice Department has gone to court over one Senate seat in Alaska, and the remedy it wants is aimed

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:34.980
at the count itself. The New York Times calls the suit highly unusual. The department stated

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.600
ground is confusion. Candidates with similar names, it argued, could taint the result.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.120
The Times reports that President Trump told the department to intervene.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:48.080
In a companion account, the Times says polling shows Republicans at risk of losing the seat.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.680
A separate Times report says legal experts see the challenge as dubious, because no obvious legal

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.440
authority supports blocking certification in advance if the Republican incumbent narrowly loses.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.380
PBS put the same threat on the air, with Jonathan K. Partt and Ramesh Poneru.

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:08.629
Ohio's race for governor is where Vivek Ramaswamy's attack ad has split Republicans,

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:14.389
the Times reports. The spot targets his Democratic rival, Amy Acton. The paper calls the

00:01:14.389 --> 00:01:19.289
advertisement brutal and says the reaction showed that even in the Trump era an attack can be seen

00:01:19.289 --> 00:01:24.709
as going too far. President Trump has defended the ad. Ohio's Republican governor has joined the

00:01:24.709 --> 00:01:29.573
backlash. California counties are the next place the Justice Department has put a demand

00:01:29.573 --> 00:01:34.153
in writing. Election officials across the state receive letters asking them to help identify

00:01:34.153 --> 00:01:38.953
non-citizen voters. The letters say that if they fail to comply, they risk significant

00:01:38.953 --> 00:01:44.473
federal scrutiny. Jeffrey Rath and Mitch Sylvester were removed from the Alberta Prosperity

00:01:44.473 --> 00:01:49.713
Project weeks ago, the Globe and Mail reports. A movement leader says the removal was for cause.

00:01:50.093 --> 00:01:54.193
That leader says the news was kept quiet so it would not derail the referendum campaign.

00:01:54.193 --> 00:02:02.621
Nursing homes in several provinces say tighter federal immigration rules now put them at risk of losing significant numbers of temporary foreign workers.

00:02:03.121 --> 00:02:13.721
The Globe and Mail reports that the homes look after seniors with cognitive problems or dementia, who need help dressing, bathing or feeding, and that local hiring often falls short, so they recruit abroad.

00:02:13.721 --> 00:02:19.221
Lisa Levin, chief executive of Advantage Ontario, which represents the province's municipal

00:02:19.221 --> 00:02:23.821
and nonprofit long-term care homes, said the policy changes have made a difficult staffing

00:02:23.821 --> 00:02:25.361
situation in healthcare worse.

00:02:26.041 --> 00:02:30.761
Mary Pollack, chief executive of the BC Care Providers Association, called the result

00:02:30.761 --> 00:02:35.141
an unintended consequence, and said the government may not have seen the scale of the impacts.

00:02:35.701 --> 00:02:40.941
The paper reports that Ottawa has moved since 2024 to curb international students in temporary

00:02:40.941 --> 00:02:45.661
foreign workers, after criticism that the influx created housing problems and made it harder

00:02:45.661 --> 00:02:47.141
for Canadians to find jobs.

00:02:47.741 --> 00:02:52.721
Leaven pointed to two measures, a labor market impact assessment, the form and employer files

00:02:52.721 --> 00:02:57.641
to confirm no Canadian is available, now lasts six months instead of a year, and each

00:02:57.641 --> 00:02:59.221
application costs $1,000.

00:02:59.921 --> 00:03:02.341
Work permits were shortened from two years to one.

00:03:02.881 --> 00:03:07.241
Gavin McIntosh, Operations and Finance Vice President for the Care Group, which runs

00:03:07.241 --> 00:03:12.401
nine facilities in British Columbia said homes are paying overtime and that it is not sustainable.

00:03:13.001 --> 00:03:17.341
Levin said other homes have turned to temporary staffing agencies, which cost more and

00:03:17.341 --> 00:03:22.301
unsettle residents who cannot communicate their own care plans, so each new worker has to be trained

00:03:22.301 --> 00:03:27.281
again. Samuel Carbano, a spokesperson for Employment and Social Development Canada,

00:03:27.601 --> 00:03:31.581
said in an email that the program should be only a last resort for short-term problems

00:03:31.581 --> 00:03:36.261
and that it is not a substitute for Canadian talent. She said assessments are individual,

00:03:36.261 --> 00:03:39.721
not based on job title, and that other pathways exist.

00:03:40.241 --> 00:03:42.141
Quebec employers have a priority process,

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:46.141
trait month simplifier, that extends to foreign personal support workers.

00:03:46.701 --> 00:03:50.641
John Francois Blanchard, Executive Director of Two Homes, West of Montreal,

00:03:51.081 --> 00:03:54.021
and President of a group representing Quebec Private Nursing Homes,

00:03:54.281 --> 00:03:59.221
said some candidates are stuck because that stream does not recognize training from private establishments.

00:03:59.561 --> 00:04:04.841
He said members could potentially lose 650 of their 6,000 workers because of immigration issues.

00:04:04.841 --> 00:04:09.301
Carbano also cited a temporary extension letting rural employers keep their

00:04:09.301 --> 00:04:12.941
foreign worker numbers until next March if they apply for new assessments.

00:04:13.521 --> 00:04:18.221
Ontario and Alberta are not participating. Ashley Hoffmeister, Human Resources

00:04:18.221 --> 00:04:22.941
Director for the District of Canora Homes for the Aged, said 28 of 400 employees

00:04:22.941 --> 00:04:27.341
in her three northwest Ontario facilities face expiring papers. She tried

00:04:27.341 --> 00:04:32.681
the significant benefit to Canada Permit, known as C10, which cost $230 and

00:04:32.681 --> 00:04:36.701
requires the employer to show a contribution significant enough to skip the assessment.

00:04:37.141 --> 00:04:41.321
She said some applications were refused because officials did not recognize the workers as

00:04:41.321 --> 00:04:42.221
healthcare personnel.

00:04:42.741 --> 00:04:46.741
A rejection letter she shared said the personal support workers appeared to be highly or

00:04:46.741 --> 00:04:50.321
mainly specific to an individual's personal care and not healthcare.

